#
# All expected_pi_string values match the canonical π reference
# (cross-validated bit-exact against mpmath.mp.pi @ dps=1100 — which
# implements Borwein-Borwein 4th-order convergent algorithm and is
# the de-facto reference for arbitrary-precision π in Python; also
# verifiable against Khinchin *Continued Fractions* §10 canonical
# CF expansion [3; 7, 15, 1, 292, 1, 1, 1, 2, 1, 3, 1, 14, 2, 1, 1,
# ...] — the convergent ladder is the substrate identity per
# [[user_stance_pi_spectral_shape_scalar_invariant]]).
#
# AST-gate: pi_cascade_digits's call graph contains zero math.pi
# invocations (enforced by tests/test_pi_cascade_primitives.py).
#
# Per `[[user_stance_pi_spectral_shape_scalar_invariant]]`: the
# decimal-expansion strings below are downstream-readout shadows of
# the substrate-level CF-convergent ladder. The catalog ships the
# decimal expansion (the readable artifact) backed by the substrate
# computation (the cascade primitive).
#
# Per `[[user_stance_pi_as_projection]]`: π is generated by a
# cascade-substrate operation; the scalar value at any digit count
# is the projection-shadow under continuous-length metric. rc13's
# expansion of the cap to 1000 digits operationalises this stance
# at a wider scale window — the same cascade, the same shape, more
# of the projection visible.

